eting compliance costs will not water down essential safety standards, because current frontier labs already operate without effective public oversight on their internal training runs. Critics argue that easing regulatory burdens on open-source projects leaves critical infrastructure vulnerable to catastrophic frontier model failures, but this assumes that closed-source proprietary systems are inherently safer. Empirical data shows that closed models from dominant firms obscure their training data and safety failures behind trade secret protections, leaving the public with less accountability than an open-source repository subject to public peer review. Binding international standards must therefore protect open-source development through explicit safe harbors while aggressively penalizing concrete deployment failures by commercial operators. National legislatures hold the fiscal and legal authority to tax excessive compute concentration directly, shifting the enforcement cost onto the firms reaping billions in monopoly profits from automated labor displacement.",
